# START HERE — pxa @@TAG@@

I wrote this page for one reader: you have a single **Tesla P100 16 GB**, a working NVIDIA
driver, and either this tarball or the container image. Nothing is built, nothing is compiled,
nothing is `pip install`ed. Most of the fifteen minutes is the model download.

**You will not export a single variable.** No `PXA_*` appears in any command on this page. The
engine reads your card at startup, fills in its own batch geometry, arms the levers that were
measured to pay on that silicon, declines the ones that were not, and prints every one of those
decisions before it serves. If some other page tells you to export a lever to get the speed
below, that page is older than this one.

Two ways in, same binaries, same one line: the **tarball** (below) or the **container**
(further down). Pick either.

---

## What you need

- **Linux, x86_64.** No macOS or Windows binaries are in this package.
- **A Pascal or Volta NVIDIA card.** Tesla P100 (`sm_60`), GTX 10-series / 1080 Ti / P40
  (`sm_61`), Tesla V100 (`sm_70`). This build is compiled for exactly those three
  architectures and will not run GPU kernels on a Turing or newer card.
- **An NVIDIA driver new enough for the bundled CUDA @@CUDA_MAJOR_MINOR@@ runtime** — driver
  **@@DRIVER_FLOOR@@** or newer on Linux. Check with `nvidia-smi` (the driver version is in the
  top-right of its banner). You do **not** need the CUDA *toolkit* installed: this package
  brings its own CUDA runtime in `lib/`. It deliberately does not bring the driver, which has
  to match your kernel. This build was packaged on driver @@BUILD_HOST_DRIVER@@.
- **glibc >= @@GLIBC_FLOOR@@** (and the package carries its own libstdc++ >= @@GLIBCXX_FLOOR@@
  in `lib/`, so only the glibc floor is yours to satisfy). Concretely: @@DISTRO_HINT@@. Check
  with `ldd --version`. This is a hard floor, not a guess — an older host fails at exec with
  `version GLIBC_x.y not found` and no flag fixes it.
- **For the container path only:** Docker plus the NVIDIA Container Toolkit (or the older
  `nvidia-docker2` runtime — both are covered below).
- **Disk:** about 20 GB free — 14 GB for the model file, the rest for this package.
- **RAM:** the weights live on the card, not in host RAM. Any box that can run a desktop is
  enough; the loader memory-maps the file rather than reading it all into memory.
- **`python3` on your PATH.** Used by the launcher and by the two measurement snippets below.
  There is nothing to install into it.

@@PATCHELF_NOTE@@

---

## Step 1 — unpack

```bash
tar xzf pxa-@@TAG@@-linux-x86_64-cuda@@CUDA_MAJOR_MINOR@@-*.tar.gz
cd pxa-@@TAG@@
```

## Step 2 — get the model, and check what you got

One file. For a single 16 GB card it is **PXA-Fusion2-35B at the PXQU-16 tier with the q8_0
output head** — a 35B mixture-of-experts model whose per-tensor mix is sized so the whole thing
sits in 16 GB of VRAM with room for context.

| | |
|---|---|
| file | `fusion2-35b-U16-q8head.gguf` |
| size | about 14 GB <!-- bench/fair-battle.md:21 "PXQU-16+q8head (14.1 GB)"; bench/HEAD-TO-HEAD.md:114 "14.0 GB" --> |
| sha256 | `643e55b07faf8b9b359fcb050494ec9c56e51c2db8d81751b4120b97782c5779` <!-- bench/fair/weights/MANIFEST.sha256, PXQU-16 q8_0-head entry; same hash in bench/checksums.sha256 --> |
| where | <https://huggingface.co/poisonxa/PXA-bench-files-GGUF/resolve/main/fusion2-35b-U16-q8head.gguf> |

```bash
curl -L -o fusion2-35b-U16-q8head.gguf \
  https://huggingface.co/poisonxa/PXA-bench-files-GGUF/resolve/main/fusion2-35b-U16-q8head.gguf
```

Now check it. The hash above is also in `bench/fair/weights/MANIFEST.sha256` inside this
package, so you can verify against the package rather than against this page:

```bash
sha256sum fusion2-35b-U16-q8head.gguf
# 643e55b07faf8b9b359fcb050494ec9c56e51c2db8d81751b4120b97782c5779  fusion2-35b-U16-q8head.gguf

# or, with the file sitting next to the manifest:
#   cd bench/fair/weights && sha256sum -c MANIFEST.sha256 --ignore-missing
```

If the two strings differ by even one character, stop and download again — a truncated download
is the usual cause, and every number below was measured on exactly these bytes.

*On an 11 GB GTX 1080 Ti instead?* Take `PXA-Fusion2-35B-PXQ2.gguf` from the same repository
(sha256 `c11b45ef4b6c2301137f07d9039287e0b85e0c6eb26fd3cb736d1d0c8f0fee4d`) and use that card's
line from `docs/COOKBOOK.md`; the rest of this page still applies, but the numbers below are the
P100's. <!-- bench/fair/weights/MANIFEST.sha256, 2-bit tier entry; docs/data/chart-data-2026-09-02.csv marks every published 1080 Ti cell "PUBLISHED tier fusion2-35b-PXQ2 (c11b45ef)" -->

## Step 3 — run one line

```bash
./run-server.sh -m fusion2-35b-U16-q8head.gguf -ngl 99 -c 8192 -fa on
```

That is the whole command. No `-b`, no `-ub`, no split mode, no cache type, no environment.
<!-- README.md:70, the 1x Tesla P100 16 GB row of "Try it in five minutes" -->

It serves on **port 8080**, bound to **127.0.0.1** — from the same machine that is all you need;
to reach it from another machine add `--host 0.0.0.0` and open the port yourself.
<!-- common/common.h:493 port = 8080; common/common.h:499 hostname = "127.0.0.1" -->

**More than one card in the box?** Then that line uses *all* of them — with `-ngl 99` the engine
spreads the layers across every card it can see, and the numbers further down are one P100's. To
hand it a single card, name the card in front of the command, using the index `nvidia-smi -L`
prints:

```bash
nvidia-smi -L
# GPU 0: Tesla P100-PCIE-16GB (UUID: GPU-aad5ef40-9b80-8fd0-4391-dfe595f42640)
# GPU 1: Tesla V100-PCIE-16GB (UUID: GPU-9af9aaf0-3f5a-cca5-b0c1-0a5b04971db6)

CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 ./run-server.sh -m fusion2-35b-U16-q8head.gguf -ngl 99 -c 8192 -fa on
```

That index means what you think it means because `run-server.sh` and `./pxa-launch` pin
`CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER=PCI_BUS_ID` for you. CUDA's own default sorts cards fastest-first, so on a box
mixing a P100 with a V100 a bare `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0` starts on the V100 while `nvidia-smi`
calls index 0 the P100 — I pin the order in both scripts so the two numberings are one numbering.
The UUID form is immune to the question either way: `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=GPU-aad5ef40-...`. In a
container it is `--gpus '"device=0"'` or `--gpus device=GPU-aad5ef40-...`; Docker counts the same
way `nvidia-smi` does. Whichever you use, confirm what you got before you measure anything — the
`pxa: dev 0 <card name>` line in the log below names the card the model is actually on.
<!-- common/common.h:328 split_mode defaults to LLAMA_SPLIT_MODE_LAYER, i.e. every visible card;
     scripts/make-release-tarball.sh (run-server.sh heredoc) and tools/pxa-launch.py device_env()
     both set CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER=PCI_BUS_ID, and both keep an order the reader exported -->

Wait for the weights to load, then prove it answers:

```bash
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/health
# {"status":"ok"}

curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/completion -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"prompt":"The capital city of France is","n_predict":16,"temperature":0,"cache_prompt":false}' \
  | python3 -c 'import json,sys; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["content"])'
```

If you would rather be asked than type flags, `./pxa-launch` does the same job: it lists your
cards, lists the model files it can find, asks what the seat is for, then prints the command and
the measurement behind every choice before it starts anything. `./pxa-launch --explain` decides
and runs nothing.

---

## This is what success looks like

**The log.** These lines go to **stderr** while the server starts, while the HTTP lines
(including `HTTP server listening`) go to stdout — so capture both, `./run-server.sh ... 2>&1 |
tee pxa.log`, if you are going to report anything. This block is the engine telling you what it
decided and why; it is the half of a bug report that matters most.

```
PXA config level: ENHANCE (default; PXA_ENHANCE=0 for DEFAULT, PXA_REFERENCE=1 for REFERENCE)
PXA level=ENHANCE | dev0 Tesla P100-PCIE-16GB(sm_60): FP16_GEMM ON [2:1 hgemm] MASK_SKIP_TILE ON [bit-exact] | mode=balance [fa-on serving] | spec: SPEC_RELAXED ON [G3, spec lanes]
pxa: engine | codec=PXQ3 (120 tensors; pxq2 41, pxq3 58, pxq4 21, pxq6 0)
pxa: dev 0 Tesla P100-PCIE-16GB cc 6.0 -> path: sm_60 fp16-hfma2
PXA posture: mode=balance [fa-on serving, decode-first] fa=on (explicit) ub=2048 (adaptive: dev0 free-min <N> MiB, min total 16384 MiB, model share <N> MiB (uniform split) -> headroom <N> MiB)
PXA_AUTO: samplers arch=qwen35moe -> temp=0.70 top_k=20 top_p=0.80 min_p=0.00 (qwen no-think agent defaults (official Qwen no-think sampler set); override: the CLI flag or PXA_AUTO_SAMPLERS=0)
PXA_AUTO: FUSE_DELTANET=53 (deltanet hybrid arch — cluster + row-absorb fusions active (bit-exact with eager; out-gate fusion off by default, guarded, see pxa/README.md); override PXA_FUSE_DELTANET)
```

<!-- wording taken verbatim from the engine source in this release:
     ggml/src/ggml-cuda/pxa/pxa-enhance.cuh (PXA config level / PXA level= / FUSE_DELTANET),
     src/llama.cpp (pxa: engine | codec= ... and pxa: dev N ... -> path:),
     examples/server/server.cpp (PXA posture: ... and PXA_AUTO: samplers ...).
     Conditions checked for THIS cell (one sm_60 card, PXQU file, arch qwen35moe, ENHANCE default):
       - level line: always printed; "default" is the reason string when no PXA_* is set.
       - the dev0 segment is the cc==600 branch; FP16_GEMM and MASK_SKIP_TILE both resolve ON at
         any level above REFERENCE; ROUTER_FUSE is not appended because it resolves to 0 here.
       - SPEC_RELAXED appears because it resolves ON at ENHANCE exactly.
       - codec=: the headline names the tier holding the most tensors and the per-tier census
         follows; the counts shown are the PXQU-16 tier map as read out in docs/LAUNCHER.md:1161.
       - samplers: arch qwen35moe has a family row, and the command line above sets no sampler
         flag, so every field is filled and printed.
       - FA_PREFILL_SPLIT does not appear: it is opt-in only and resolves to 0. -->

Hold this page to the exact values of three things only: the level (`ENHANCE`), the flags on the
`dev0` line, and `ub=2048`. The `<N> MiB` figures belong to your card, and the tensor counts
belong to your copy of the file.

Two lines deserve a word. `codec=` names the tier this file carries — if it says `codec=off` you
are not running a PXQ file at all. `ub=2048` is the micro-batch the engine chose for your card by
itself; it is also the geometry the published numbers below were measured at, so if you see a
smaller number there, something else is already resident on the card.

There is **no** `PXA_AUTO: batch defaults for 1x P100` line, and its absence is correct: the
measured per-card batch table has cells for a 2× P100 pair, a 2× V100 pair, four P100s and a
single 1080 Ti, and a single P100 is not one of them, so the adaptive ladder does the choosing
and the `PXA posture:` line above reports it. <!-- ggml/src/ggml-cuda.cu, ggml_backend_cuda_pxa_suggest_batch: cells are 2x sm_70, 2x sm_60, 1x sm_61, 4x sm_60 -->

**One line may or may not appear**, depending on how much of your card is free — the drafter is
armed only when the estimate leaves at least 2 GiB after the weights:

```
PXA_AUTO: spec arch=qwen35moe -> --spec-type ngram-mod:n_max=4,n_min=2 (measured +23.0% first-request decode on code traffic ...)
```
```
PXA_AUTO: spec DECLINED -- only dev0 free <N> MiB, model share <N> MiB (uniform split) -> headroom <N> MiB, under the 2048 MiB a draft context needs (override with --spec-type, or PXA_AUTO_SPEC=1 to force)
```

Either is a healthy boot. On a card with nothing else on it, expect the first: a 14 GB file on a
16 GB card leaves a little over 2 GiB, so the drafter usually arms, a `PXA_SPEC_RELAXED:` line
follows it, and completion responses then carry `draft_n` / `draft_n_accepted` in their
`timings`. It drafts only when it can match an n-gram, so it costs nothing when it cannot.
<!-- examples/server/server.cpp: the AUTO-SPEC block, its 2 GiB headroom gate and the qwen35moe row;
     examples/server/server-task.cpp: draft_n / draft_n_accepted appear in timings only when a stage is armed -->

**The numbers.** These two are the floor this card is published at — meet them or beat them:

| | floor for 1× Tesla P100 16 GB | how it was measured |
|---|---|---|
| **decode** | **62 t/s or better** | server-reported `timings.predicted_per_second`, 200-token generations from a short prompt, model fully GPU-resident, median of 3 <!-- published 62.4, and 63.0 with the residual-add fusion that now ships on: docs/COOKBOOK.md:96-97; bench/README.md:52-58; docs/LAUNCHER.md:174 --> |
| **prefill** | **817 t/s or better** at `ub=2048` | one **cold ~5,800-token prompt**, same server, same geometry <!-- published 827-843: bench/README.md:58; docs/COOKBOOK.md:97. The floor is the lowest published reading on this file at ub2048, 816.8 t/s, from the session in bench/fair-battle.md:79 --> |
| first token | *not a published number* | whatever `timings.prompt_ms` says for the prompt **you** sent; it scales with your prompt length, so it is reported here, never compared |

**Prefill is measured on a long cold prompt on purpose.** A short prompt reports a much smaller
prefill rate because the fixed per-request cost dominates — that is not a fault, and it is not
comparable to the floor above.

Measure decode with three runs and take the median. **Every run gets its own prompt**: the
engine's n-gram drafter learns text it has already seen, so re-sending one prompt makes the rate
climb run over run and measures the cache rather than the card (`bench/fair/protocol.md` rule 6):

```bash
for i in 1 2 3; do
  python3 -c 'import json,random,sys; random.seed(int(sys.argv[1])); print(json.dumps({"prompt":"Write a detailed technical summary of how a GPU executes a matrix multiply. Reference case %d." % random.randrange(999999),"n_predict":200,"temperature":0,"cache_prompt":False}))' "$i" > /tmp/pxa-decode.json
  curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/completion -H 'Content-Type: application/json' --data @/tmp/pxa-decode.json \
  | python3 -c 'import json,sys; t=json.load(sys.stdin)["timings"]; print("decode %.1f t/s   first token %.0f ms" % (t["predicted_per_second"], t["prompt_ms"]))'
done
```

Measure prefill with one long, cold prompt. The word count below is a starting point — the
number that matters is `prompt_n` in the reply, so raise or lower `range(1800)` until it comes
back near **5,800**, and keep it under 8,000 or it will not fit the `-c 8192` context. Change
the seed between repeats so no two prompts are ever the same:

```bash
python3 -c 'import json,random; random.seed(11); print(json.dumps({"prompt":" ".join(f"item{random.randrange(9999)}" for _ in range(1800)),"n_predict":8,"temperature":0,"cache_prompt":False}))' > /tmp/pxa-long.json
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/completion -H 'Content-Type: application/json' --data @/tmp/pxa-long.json \
  | python3 -c 'import json,sys; t=json.load(sys.stdin)["timings"]; print("prompt_n %d   prefill %.1f t/s" % (t["prompt_n"], t["prompt_per_second"]))'
```

**Did it reproduce?** The protocol these numbers are held to is in `bench/fair/protocol.md`:
temperature 0 (rule 2), **one warmup run discarded and the median of 7 reported** (rule 3), a
**unique prompt for every repeat** with `cache_prompt` false (rule 6). Do that, and your median
decode should be **at least 62 t/s** and your median prefill **at least 817 t/s**. Higher is
expected: those figures were taken on an earlier build, they are a floor rather than a band, and
I re-measure them for each tag — beating them is the engine getting faster, not your run going
wrong. A first request is always slower than the rest; that is what rule 3's discarded warmup is
for. If a median lands *below* its floor, the next section is for you.

---

## The container path

Same binaries, same one line, no unpacking. It needs Docker with the NVIDIA Container Toolkit.
Step 2 still applies — models are not in the image; put the file you downloaded and checked in
the directory you mount at `/models`.

```bash
docker run -d --name pxa \
    --gpus '"device=0"' \
    -p 8080:8080 \
    -v /path/to/your/models:/models:ro \
    ghcr.io/poisonxa16/pxa:@@TAG@@ \
    -m /models/fusion2-35b-U16-q8head.gguf \
    -ngl 99 -c 8192 -fa on
```
<!-- shape and flags from docker/README.md "Quick start — one card (P100 or V100)"; image tag pinned as in README.md's container block -->

If your Docker predates the `--gpus` flag and uses the older `nvidia-docker2` runtime, swap that
one argument: `--runtime=nvidia -e NVIDIA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0`.

The image binds `0.0.0.0` inside the container, so `-p 8080:8080` is what makes it reachable —
the same `curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/health` proves it. Read the log with `docker logs pxa`; it
contains exactly the lines quoted above. If you move it off port 8080, change it in **two**
places — `--port N` for the server and `-e LLAMA_ARG_PORT=N` so the image's built-in health
check probes the same place.

---

## When it does not look like that

**It will not start at all: `version GLIBC_2.xx not found` (or `GLIBCXX_...`).** Your distro is
older than the floor in "What you need". `ldd --version` tells you what you have; the floor for
the build you downloaded is in the `VERSION` file next to this page. There is no flag for this —
it is a newer distro, or a container with a newer userspace. Nothing is wrong with your card.

**It starts, then: `CUDA driver version is insufficient for CUDA runtime version`.** The driver
is older than @@DRIVER_FLOOR@@. Upgrade the *driver*; do not install a CUDA toolkit — this
package already carries its own CUDA runtime.

**In a container: `could not select device driver "" with capabilities: [[gpu]]`, or the server
starts but no `pxa: dev 0 ...` line ever appears and everything is glacial.** Docker did not
hand the card through. Two different mechanisms exist and only one works on any given host:
`--gpus '"device=0"'` (NVIDIA Container Toolkit) or `--runtime=nvidia -e
NVIDIA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0` (older nvidia-docker2). Try the other one. The first line of the log
says which world you are in (`PXA_CONTAINER_AWARE_v1: runtime=container`), and `nvidia-smi`
*inside* the container is the direct test.

**The file you downloaded is not called what the recipes call it.** Everything here and in
`docs/COOKBOOK.md` names `fusion2-35b-U16-q8head.gguf`. A browser or mirror may hand you a
different filename — rename it, or point `-m` at whatever it is actually called; nothing reads
the name except you. Do not confuse it with `fusion2-35b-U16.gguf` (same tier, different output
head, different bytes) or with the `PXA-Fusion4-*` files, which are a different model.

**`sha256sum` does not match.** Download it again. That mismatch means these bytes are not the
bytes any published number was taken on, so nothing below the download is worth debugging until
it matches. A partial download is by far the most common cause; a mirror that "helpfully"
recompressed the file is the second.

**The launcher asks something this page did not mention.** `./pxa-launch` asks three things —
which cards, which model, and what the seat is for (chat / serving several people / long
documents). That last one is the only performance question a human is asked, because on these
cards it genuinely changes the answer; pick *chat* for the run described here. If it instead
asks you to **acknowledge an unmeasured branch**, you are not on the cell this page describes:
for one P100 plus this PXQU-16 file it matches its `1xp100-pxqu16` row and waives that
acknowledgement, printing the reason. Check that `-m` really points at
`fusion2-35b-U16-q8head.gguf` and that only one card is ticked. `./run-server.sh` never asks
anything at all — use it if you want no questions.

**Something answers, but not on the port you expected.** The server prints `HTTP server
listening` with the host and port it actually bound. The tarball defaults to `127.0.0.1:8080`,
so a `curl` from another machine gets nothing until you pass `--host 0.0.0.0`. In a container
the bind is `0.0.0.0` and the mapping (`-p 8080:8080`) is what you change instead. If you pass
`--port`, remember the container needs `-e LLAMA_ARG_PORT` to match, and note that on this
engine a `LLAMA_ARG_*` variable overrides the matching command-line flag rather than the other
way round — so set a port in one place, never in both.

**It loads and then dies on the first token, or fails while loading weights.** The model tier
does not fit alongside its context and compute buffer. Confirm the card is otherwise empty
(`nvidia-smi`), lower `-c`, or step down a tier. On 16 GB, PXQU-16 is the tier that fits; the
4-bit flagship (18.7 GB) does not fit one 16 GB card, whatever its filename suggests.

**Everything works but a number is under its floor.** Re-read the `PXA posture:` line: if `ub` is
not 2048, something else is resident on the card and the engine sized down to stay safe. And
check what you measured — a short prompt cannot produce a four-figure prefill rate, only a cold
prompt of a few thousand tokens can. A number *above* its floor needs no explanation and is not a
fault: the floors come from an earlier build.

---

## Where things are

```
bin/            llama-server, llama-cli, llama-bench, llama-perplexity, llama-quantize,
                llama-pxq-export (turns a PXQ file back into a plain GGUF any stock llama.cpp
                reads -- docs/PXQ-EXPORT.md, and the two-command recipe in docs/COOKBOOK.md),
                (llama-gguf-split if this build included it), and three self-test binaries.
lib/            this engine's shared libraries + the exact CUDA/OpenMP runtime libs it needs.
                Driver libraries are deliberately NOT here — those come from your host.
pxa-launch      lists your cards and models, picks a configuration, prints its reasoning.
run-server.sh   thin wrapper: sets up ./lib, execs bin/llama-server with your own flags.
tools/pxa-launch.py   what pxa-launch runs (kept here so --explain works).
bench/fair/     the comparison protocol + weights/MANIFEST.sha256 (file names and hashes).
bench/gate/     the release gate — determinism, coherence, needle recall, unit tests:
                LD_LIBRARY_PATH=./lib MODEL=/path/to/model.gguf BIN=./bin bench/gate/run-gate.sh
                (it calls bin/llama-server directly, so it needs ./lib on the path itself;
                pxa-launch and run-server.sh do that for you, run-gate.sh does not).
docs/           README, this release's notes, COOKBOOK (per-card recipes), KNOWN-ISSUES,
                PXQ-EXPORT (leaving PXQ: what bin/llama-pxq-export does and its flags),
                LAUNCHER (the decision table and worked examples).
VERSION         the commit, build flags, CUDA version, GPU architectures and library floors
                of the exact build you have.
LICENSE, LICENSING.md   MIT-family licences; LICENSING.md explains which files fall where.
```

If a number on this page does not reproduce from the same command on the same file, that is a
bug report worth filing — with the `PXA config level:` line, the whole `PXA_AUTO:` block, your
`nvidia-smi` output and the command you ran.
